== plan and requests ==
accepted plan.md (accepted/plan/visit-1/attempt-1/plan.md):
# Plan: Implement `slugify`

## Goal

Implement `slugify(text)` in `src/slugify.mjs` and add tests in
`test/slugify.test.mjs` using `node:test`, so that:

- input is lowercased
- any run of one or more characters that are not `a`-`z` or `0`-`9` becomes a
  single hyphen
- the result has no leading or trailing hyphens
- `node --test` passes

## Current state

- `src/slugify.mjs` exists and exports a stub:

  ```js
  export function slugify(text) {
    throw new Error("not implemented");
  }
  ```

- There is no `test/` directory yet.
- `package.json` is `{"name":"slug-fixture","type":"module","private":true}`
  — no `test` script and no test runner dependency declared. The repo is an
  ESM package (`"type": "module"`), so all new files must use `import`/
  `export` syntax and a `.mjs` (or `.js`, given `type: module`) extension.
- Node available in this environment is v26.7.0, which has a built-in
  `node:test` module — no dependency needs to be added.

## Step-by-step implementation

### 1. Implement `slugify` in `src/slugify.mjs`

Replace the stub body with an implementation that:

1. Converts `text` to a string and lowercases it (`String(text).toLowerCase()`).
2. Replaces every maximal run of characters outside `[a-z0-9]` with a single
   `-` (regex: `/[^a-z0-9]+/g` applied after lowercasing).
3. Strips any leading and trailing `-` from the result
   (regex: `/^-+|-+$/g`, or `.replace(/^-|-$/g, ...)` in a loop — the `+`
   quantifier form handles it in one pass).

Reference implementation:

```js
export function slugify(text) {
  return String(text)
    .toLowerCase()
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, "-")
    .replace(/^-+|-+$/g, "");
}
```

Notes:
- Order matters: lowercase first, then collapse non-alphanumeric runs, then
  trim hyphens. Trimming must happen after collapsing so an original
  leading/trailing run of punctuation (which collapses to a single `-`) is
  removed rather than left as a boundary hyphen.
- No external dependencies are needed; this is pure `String`/`RegExp` usage.
- Keep the exported function signature `slugify(text)` unchanged so existing
  imports (e.g., from tests) keep working.

### 2. Add tests in `test/slugify.test.mjs`

Create the `test/` directory and add a test file using `node:test` and
`node:assert/strict`, covering each acceptance criterion explicitly:

```js
import { test } from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
import { slugify } from "../src/slugify.mjs";

test("lowercases its input", () => {
  assert.equal(slugify("HELLO"), "hello");
});

test("collapses runs of non a-z0-9 characters into one hyphen", () => {
  assert.equal(slugify("Hello, World!!"), "hello-world");
  assert.equal(slugify("a___b---c"), "a-b-c");
  assert.equal(slugify("foo   bar"), "foo-bar");
});

test("has no leading or trailing hyphens", () => {
  assert.equal(slugify("  Hello World  "), "hello-world");
  assert.equal(slugify("---leading and trailing---"), "leading-and-trailing");
  assert.equal(slugify("!!!"), "");
});

test("keeps digits and letters, mixed case and separators", () => {
  assert.equal(slugify("Node.js 20 Release!"), "node-js-20-release");
});
```

- Import path is relative (`../src/slugify.mjs`) since there's no package
  export map; this matches the `type: module` ESM setup.
